// Writes std/text/ucd.calx, the Unicode tables std.regex reads, from the Unicode Character
// Database's own files, which it downloads from unicode.org into build/ucd/<version> the first
// time, and reads from there after.
//
//     calx run tools/unicode/gen_ucd.calx
//
// The tables are Unicode 16's, as Java 25 has it:
//
// - WORD, `\w` as UTS #18 defines it: Alphabetic, marks, decimal digits, connector punctuation and
//   Join_Control. Alphabetic is the letters, letter numbers and Other_Alphabetic, whose runes are
//   all marks, which WORD has anyway, except the circled and squared Latin letters.
// - DIGIT, `\d`: the decimal digits, Nd.
// - SPACE, `\s`: White_Space.
// - FOLD, simple case folding, as orbits: each rune that folds with others, and the next one in its
//   orbit, the smallest past it or else the smallest of all, as Go's unicode.SimpleFold gives. A
//   rune folds with its full case folding when that's one rune, CaseFolding.txt's C entries, and
//   otherwise with its full lowercase when that's one rune, as an S entry has it, so the dotless i,
//   whose only folding is Turkic, folds with nothing.
import std.env
import std.fs
import std.http
import std.path
import std.process

const VERSION: str = "16.0.0"
const MAX: u32 = 0x10FFFF

fn notice() -> String {
